---
// Lesson 11.4c — MC Practice 3 + Written-Response Drill
// assessment: mixed set 3 under time + timed WR block; full error table v3.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderMcqSet } from '../../lib/mc-quiz';

const KEYS = '../data/mcq-keys.json';

const body = `<h1>11.4c — MC Practice 3 + Written-Response Drill</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The last practice before exam eve: a mixed set under compressed
    time, then a timed written-response block with your PPR — the
    same 60-minute pairing the real exam gives you, in miniature.
    Today you rehearse STAMINA, not just facts.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderMcqSet(3, KEYS)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Error Table v3</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Tighter than real:</strong> 13 minutes for 15
    questions, where the exam gives you 120 for 70. That 50-second
    target deliberately overtrains pass-1 speed — if you can bubble
    at this pace here, May 14 will feel slow. The three-pass method
    is REQUIRED, not optional (bubbling discipline now, so it is
    reflex on exam day).</p>
    <p>Score → error table v3, cumulative across sets 1–3. Your
    REAL study plan for the weekend comes from this table, not
    from your feelings about the exam.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">WR Block</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Timed, PPR in hand, phone in bag:</strong> 4 prompts
    in 25 minutes (exam gives 60 for 4 with more writing — we
    compress to build the muscle):</p>
    <ol>
        <li>MCQ-style: foreign code excerpt — describe input,
            behavior, output (reading pseudocode under time, not
            a WR prompt — <a href="11.3.html">11.3</a> drill two)</li>
        <li>WR2a-style: your algorithm, from your PPR</li>
        <li>WR2b-style: your difficulty + specific fix</li>
        <li>WR2c-style: your list's role in the program</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Trade and grade with the claim-evidence-because
    rubric (<a href="11.3.html">11.3</a>):</strong> does every
    answer name its evidence? Does anyone's WR2b name all three
    (bug, cause, fix)? Score each other like the College Board
    would — generous grading in practice is mean in May.</p>


    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Run pass-1 speed under 60 seconds per question</li>
            <li>Complete 4 WR prompts inside 25 minutes with evidence</li>
            <li>Show error tables v1–v3 and name your two weak families</li>
            <li>Write your weekend plan from the table, not the vibes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/mc-strategy.html">MC Strategy</a> — pass timing targets</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/written-responses.html">Written Responses</a> — the rubric you graded with</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/reference-sheet.html">Exam Reference Sheet</a> — print it, hold it, love it</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.5.html">11.5 — Exam Eve: Logistics &amp; Strategy</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.4c" title="11.4c MC Practice 3 + Written-Response Drill — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
